Reason:
The SSR process did not have checks to prevent overwriting warm node blocks, which are part of a node chain that should be preserved since the last checkpoint. The condition checks if the segment type is `CURSEG_WARM_NODE`, but without proper handling, it could still overwrite the warm node blocks, leading to potential data loss or corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* don't overwrite by SSR to keep node chain */
if (se->type == CURSEG_WARM_NODE) {
    if (!f2fs_test_and_set_bit(offset, se->ckpt_valid_map))
        se->ckpt_valid_blocks++;
}
```
Reason:
The added comment and the condition ensure that when the segment type is `CURSEG_WARM_NODE`, the code will not overwrite the node blocks by setting the `ckpt_valid_map` bit. This prevents the SSR process from overwriting warm node blocks, thus preserving the integrity of the node chain since the last checkpoint.

